  aria-labelledby='case-lumastays-title'
>
  <div class='rows'>
    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-header'>
        <p class='case-eyebrow'>Web Development</p>
        <h1 id='case-lumastays-title'>Luma Stays</h1>
        <p class='case-subtitle'>
          A complete accommodation booking app built for customers and venue managers.
        </p>

        <dl class='case-summary' aria-label='Project summary'>
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>UX/UI design and front-end development</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Individual Project Exam 2</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Year</dt>
            <dd>2026</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>React, JavaScript, Bootstrap, Noroff API</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>Grade A</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class='tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper'>
          <div class='demo-links' data-auto='demo' data-case='lumastays'></div>
        </div>

        <nav
          class='project-switcher'
          aria-label='Luma Stays sections'
          data-auto='switcher'
          data-case='lumastays'
        ></nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>The brief</h2>
      <p>
        The task was to turn the Holidaze API into a complete accommodation booking app. Customers
        needed to find venues, check availability, make bookings, and manage upcoming stays. Venue
        managers needed a different set of tools for creating listings, editing venue details, and
        reviewing bookings. Both journeys had to live within the same application without making the
        interface feel crowded or confusing.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            The application supports public visitors, registered customers, and venue managers. What
            someone can see or do depends on their account type, authentication state, and
            relationship to a venue. Booking availability also changes with dates and guest numbers.
            The main challenge was keeping those rules accurate while giving each user a clear path
            through the app.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Support customer and manager journeys without building two separate interfaces</li>
            <li>Keep venue, profile, and booking data accurate after API mutations</li>
            <li>Handle loading, validation, empty states, and API errors without breaking the flow</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--explore'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/site-lumastays-600.webp'
            data-src='site-lumastays-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='484'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Luma Stays explore page showing accommodation cards, search, and filter interface'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>Explore stays with search and filtering</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--decisions'>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              I organised the app into routed React views and reusable components for venue cards,
              forms, navigation, feedback messages, and protected actions. Shared patterns stay
              consistent, while each page keeps control of its own task.
            </li>
            <li>
              The interface checks authentication, account type, and venue ownership before showing
              protected controls. Customers see booking actions, while managers get the tools needed
              to create and maintain their venues.
            </li>
            <li>
              Search matches several parts of the venue data rather than relying on the title alone.
              Guest filtering is handled separately, which makes the results more useful even when
              the API content is inconsistent.
            </li>
            <li>
              Forms preserve the user’s input when a request fails and provide clear feedback for
              validation, authentication, bookings, profile changes, and venue updates. Successful
              actions also refresh the relevant data so the interface reflects the latest API state.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--booking'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/web-lumastays-booking-600.webp'
            data-src='web-lumastays-booking-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='484'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Luma Stays venue detail page showing booking options and guest selection'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>Venue details and booking flow</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row case-outcome'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        Luma Stays received a
        <strong>Grade A</strong>
        and delivers complete customer and venue-manager journeys in one responsive React
        application. Building it gave me solid experience with authentication, role-based access,
        routed views, API mutations, and state that changes across several parts of an interface. It
        also confirmed something I care about in my work: complex functionality does not need to
        look complex. Good structure and clear feedback can keep a demanding booking flow calm and
        easy to follow.
      </p>
    </div>

